+### `bikeshed/src/main/java/com/yourdomain/bikeshed/isam/IsamMetaFileReader.java`
+
+```java
+package com.yourdomain.bikeshed.isam;
+
+import com.yourdomain.bikeshed.core.Series;
+import com.yourdomain.bikeshed.cursor.ColumnMeta;
+import com.yourdomain.bikeshed.cursor.TypeMemento;
+import com.yourdomain.bikeshed.isam.meta.IOMemento;
+
+import java.io.IOException;
+import java.nio.file.Files;
+import java.nio.file.Path;
+import java.util.ArrayList;
+import java.util.List;
+import java.util.Map;
+import java.util.Objects;
+import java.util.function.Function;
+import java.util.stream.Collectors;
+
+/**
+ * Manages reading and writing of ISAM metadata files.
+ * The metadata file defines the schema of an ISAM data file, including
+ * column names, data types, and byte offsets within each record.
+ */
+public class IsamMetaFileReader implements AutoCloseable {
+
+    private final String metafileFilename;
+    private List<RecordMeta> constraintsInternal; // Internal mutable list
+    private Series<RecordMeta> constraints; // Exposed as immutable Series
+    private int recordLength = -1; // Cached record length
+
+    public IsamMetaFileReader(String metafileFilename) {
+        this.metafileFilename = Objects.requireNonNull(metafileFilename);
+    }
+
+    /**
+     * Initializes the reader by parsing the metadata file.
+     * This method must be called before accessing {@code getConstraints()} or {@code getRecordLength()}.
+     *
+     * @throws IOException if the metafile cannot be read.
+     * @throws IllegalStateException if the metafile format is invalid.
+     */
+    public void open() throws IOException {
+        if (constraintsInternal != null) {
+            return; // Already open/parsed
+        }
+
+        List<String> lines = Files.readAllLines(Path.of(metafileFilename));
+        List<String> filteredLines = lines.stream()
+                .filter(line -> !line.trim().startsWith("#") && !line.trim().isEmpty())
+                .collect(Collectors.toList());
+
+        if (filteredLines.size() < 3) {
+            throw new IllegalStateException("Invalid metafile format: expected at least 3 non-comment lines.");
+        }
+
+        String[] coordsStr = filteredLines.get(0).trim().split("\\s+");
+        String[] names = filteredLines.get(1).trim().split("\\s+");
+        String[] types = filteredLines.get(2).trim().split("\\s+");
+
+        if (names.length != types.length || coordsStr.length != names.length * 2) {
+            throw new IllegalStateException("Metafile format mismatch: inconsistent number of columns in header lines.");
+        }
+
+        List<RecordMeta> parsedConstraints = new ArrayList<>();
+        for (int i = 0; i < names.length; i++) {
+            String name = names[i];
+            String typeName = types[i];
+            int begin = Integer.parseInt(coordsStr[2 * i]);
+            int end = Integer.parseInt(coordsStr[2 * i + 1]);
+
+            IOMemento ioMemento;
+            try {
+                ioMemento = IOMemento.valueOf(typeName);
+            } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
+                throw new IllegalStateException("Unknown IOMemento type: " + typeName, e);
+            }
+
+            // Create decoder and encoder functions based on IOMemento and calculated length
+            Function<byte[], Object> decoder = ioMemento.createDecoder(end - begin);
+            Function<Object, byte[]> encoder = ioMemento.createEncoder(end - begin);
+
+            parsedConstraints.add(new RecordMeta(name, ioMemento, begin, end, decoder, encoder));
+        }
+
+        this.constraintsInternal = parsedConstraints;
+        // Expose as an immutable Series from the internal list
+        this.constraints = Series.of(parsedConstraints.size(), parsedConstraints::get);
+        this.recordLength = parsedConstraints.isEmpty() ? 0 : parsedConstraints.get(parsedConstraints.size() - 1).getEnd();
+    }
+
+    /**
+     * Gets the collection of {@link RecordMeta} constraints defined in the metadata file.
+     *
+     * @return A {@code Series<RecordMeta>} representing the schema.
+     * @throws IllegalStateException if {@code open()} has not been called.
+     */
+    public Series<RecordMeta> getConstraints() {
+        if (constraints == null) {
+            throw new IllegalStateException("IsamMetaFileReader has not been opened. Call open() first.");
+        }
+        return constraints;
+    }
+
+    /**
+     * Gets the total record length in bytes as defined by the metadata.
+     *
+     * @return The record length.
+     * @throws IllegalStateException if {@code open()} has not been called.
+     */
+    public int getRecordLength() {
+        if (recordLength == -1) {
+            throw new IllegalStateException("IsamMetaFileReader has not been opened. Call open() first.");
+        }
+        return recordLength;
+    }
+
+    @Override
+    public void close() {
+        // No-op for this in-memory representation, but useful for actual file handles
+        System.out.println("Closing metafile reader: " + metafileFilename);
+    }
+
+    @Override
+    public String toString() {
+        return "IsamMetaFileReader(metafileFilename='" + metafileFilename + "', recordlen=" + recordLength + ", constraints=" + constraints + ")";
+    }
+
+    /**
+     * Writes metadata for an ISAM file to a specified file.
+     *
+     * @param metafileFilename The path to the metadata file to write.
+     * @param columnMetas      A Series of {@link ColumnMeta} objects defining the schema.
+     * @param varChars         A map for variable-length string columns, specifying their fixed length in bytes for this ISAM file.
+     *                         Key: column name, Value: fixed length in bytes.
+     * @return A {@code Series<RecordMeta>} with calculated offsets, representing the canonical schema for the data file.
+     * @throws IOException if the file cannot be written.
+     * @throws IllegalArgumentException if variable-length types don't have specified lengths.
+     */
+    public static Series<RecordMeta> write(String metafileFilename, Series<ColumnMeta> columnMetas, Map<String, Integer> varChars) throws IOException {
+        List<String> lines = new ArrayList<>();
+        List<RecordMeta> results = new ArrayList<>();
+
+        int currentOffset = 0;
+        List<String> coordStrings = new ArrayList<>();
+        List<String> nameStrings = new ArrayList<>();
+        List<String> typeStrings = new ArrayList<>();
+
+        for (int i = 0; i < columnMetas.size(); i++) {
+            ColumnMeta cm = columnMetas.get(i);
+            String name = cm.getName();
+            TypeMemento type = cm.getType(); // This is the TypeMemento interface
+
+            int length;
+            if (type.getNetworkSize() != null) {
+                length = type.getNetworkSize();
+            } else {
+                // Handle variable-length types
+                Integer varLength = varChars.get(name);
+                if (varLength == null) {
+                    throw new IllegalArgumentException("Variable-length column '" + name + "' does not have a specified length in varChars map.");
+                }
+                length = varLength;
+            }
+
+            int endOffset = currentOffset + length;
+            IOMemento ioType = (IOMemento) type; // Cast to IOMemento is necessary for decoder/encoder creation
+
+            Function<byte[], Object> decoder = ioType.createDecoder(length);
+            Function<Object, byte[]> encoder = ioType.createEncoder(length);
+
+            RecordMeta recordMeta = new RecordMeta(name, ioType, currentOffset, endOffset, decoder, encoder);
+            results.add(recordMeta);
+
+            coordStrings.add(String.valueOf(currentOffset));
+            coordStrings.add(String.valueOf(endOffset));
+            nameStrings.add(name);
+            typeStrings.add(ioType.name()); // Use enum name for type string
+
+            currentOffset = endOffset;
+        }
+
+        lines.add("# format: coords WS .. EOL names WS .. EOL TypeMemento WS ..");
+        lines.add("# last coord is the recordlen");
+        lines.add(String.join(" ", coordStrings));
+        lines.add(String.join(" ", nameStrings));
+        lines.add(String.join(" ", typeStrings));
+
+        Files.write(Path.of(metafileFilename), lines);
+        return Series.of(results.size(), results::get);
+    }
+}
+```
+
+---
+
+### Trading Simulator DSEL Integration
+
+The DSEL directly supports the concurrent trading simulator requirements as follows:
+
+1.  **Binance Archive Ingestion (into ISAM structures):**
+    *   **`bbcursive` Role:** The `bbcursive` library provides the low-level parsing combinators (`Cursive`, `BBAtom`, `BBCombinator`) required to directly read and parse raw `ByteBuffer` streams. This is crucial for handling binary historical data archives (e.g., from Binance, which might be in compressed formats like Parquet, or custom binary formats, often with fixed-size records or delimited streams). Instead of reading into intermediate Java objects, `bbcursive` can define a grammar that directly extracts fields from the `ByteBuffer` in a zero-copy manner.
+    *   **`bikeshed.isam` Role:** `IsamDataFile` and `IsamMetaFileReader` would utilize `bbcursive` internally.
+        *   `IsamMetaFileReader` defines the fixed schema (column name, type, byte offset, length) for market data (timestamp, open, high, low, close, volume). `IOMemento` entries explicitly specify `networkSize` for direct alignment.
+        *   `IsamDataFile.write` (and `append`) would accept a `Cursor` (of `RowVec`s representing market ticks) and efficiently write them into the ISAM binary format, using `bbcursive`'s byte-level operations to write each field directly to the memory-mapped buffer.
+        *   `IsamDataFile.read` (or its internal accessors) would use the `RecordMeta` definitions and `bbcursive` parsers to directly read and decode market tick data from the memory-mapped file into `RowVec` instances (which are then aggregated into a `Cursor`). This ensures zero-copy access from disk into DSEL structures.
+    *   **`Series` and `Cursor` for Time-Series:** The `Cursor` (which is a `Series<RowVec>`) is the natural fit for time-series data. Each `RowVec` is a single market tick. `Series` implicitly supports memory-mapped files (mmap) because its core is a `size` and an `IntFunction<T>` (or `LongFunction<T>`). For ISAM files, this `IntFunction` would simply access the underlying `MappedByteBuffer` at a calculated offset (`rowIndex * recordLength + fieldOffset`). This allows the entire dataset to be "opened" without being fully loaded into RAM.
+
+2.  **Concurrent Trade Agents on Shared, Immutable Timeline:**
+    *   **Data Access via `Series` and `Cursor`:** Agents would receive market data as `Series<MarketTick>` or `Cursor<MarketTick>`, where `MarketTick` is a `RowVec` (or a similar DSEL record type built from `Join`). These `Series` and `Cursor` instances are inherently immutable at their core (`Join` and `Series.ImmutableSeries`).
+    *   **Zero-Copy and Concurrency:** Because `Series` and `Cursor` provide a functional view over potentially memory-mapped ISAM files (`bbcursive` doing the heavy lifting), multiple agents can safely read the *same underlying data* concurrently without contention. Reads are zero-copy, minimizing GC pressure and maximizing throughput. The immutability of the `MarketTick` objects presented to agents means agents don't need locks to read market data.
+    *   **Agent-Local State:** Agent actions (e.g., placing orders, updating internal models) and wallet state updates (`WalletState`) would be managed in *agent-local* or *transaction-specific* mutable state. This aligns with the DSEL's philosophy: immutable data flow for observation, mutable state for computation, and then compositional integration of results.
+    *   **Time-series Navigation:** `Cursor` operations like `row(index)`, `get(columnNames)`, and `slice(start, end)` directly support agents needing to retrieve historical data based on their lookback periods. For example, an agent could request `marketData.slice(currentTimeIndex - lookbackPeriod, currentTimeIndex)` to get its relevant historical window.
+
+3.  **Reward-Based Agent Visibility:**
+    *   **Predicates on DSEL Primitives:** The DSEL enables constructing and evaluating predicates on agent performance records (which themselves can be `Join`-based DSEL structures). For instance, if `AgentPerformance` is a `Series<Join<AgentId, PerformanceMetrics>>`, one could `filter` it: `D.filter(allAgents, agent -> agent.getSnd().reward() > threshold)`.
+    *   **Dynamic Filtering of `Cursor` Views:** The result of such predicates (a `Series<Boolean>` or a `Series<AgentId>`) can then be used to dynamically filter what parts of the `Cursor` (e.g., which asset pairs, which time periods) are provided to a specific agent. This would involve creating a new `Cursor` instance that wraps the original but applies a dynamic `filter` or `slice` operation based on the visibility rules. The immutability of `Cursor` means this filtering creates a *new view* without modifying the underlying data, maintaining safety and clarity.
+
+This design effectively separates the concerns: `bbcursive` handles the raw byte efficiency, `bikeshed` provides the high-level immutable DSEL for data representation and manipulation, and the composite nature of `Join`/`Series`/`Cursor` directly maps to the needs of a concurrent, high-performance trading simulator with time-series data.

